Kernel Stack Slab Allocator
===========================

Per-process kernel stacks are 16KB (four contiguous physical pages).
Earlier 8KB / 2-page kstacks were too small once a process took a
syscall path that itself called into a server: the SP_EL1 grow-down
trace got close to the bottom of the lower page, and on aarch64 the
slab made adjacent slots physically contiguous, so any overflow
clobbered the *next* slot's saved exception frame at offsets
0xF0/0xF8/0x108 — the precise bytes the scheduler reads to ERET.

The chunk-3 follow-up worked around the fragmentation with a
256-page linear scan that handed out non-contiguous neighbours;
that masked overflow because writes past kstack_base just hit a
free-list page nobody was using. The slab restores PA contiguity,
so it surfaces the real overflow — pump the per-slot reservation
to 16KB and the headroom is comfortable for any realistic syscall
chain.

Strategy: at boot, drain `MAX_KSTACKS` × 4 contiguous pages from
the bump pool while it is still pristine — the bump pool returns
sequential PAs, so each page lands at `base + i * PAGE_SIZE`.
Track an in-use bit per slot. `kstack_alloc` returns the lower PA
of a free slot; `kstack_free` clears the bit.

Sized to match `MAX_THREADS` (32) so exhaustion is impossible
until that ceiling lifts; both should be bumped together when
the time comes.

    module oskit.kernel

    import oskit.config.{PAGE_SIZE}
    import oskit.arch.{page_alloc_zero, arch_irq_save, arch_irq_restore, abort}

Pool table — base PA of each 16KB chunk, plus a parallel in-use
flag. Using literals (32, not `MAX_THREADS`) for the array size
because module-level array bounds don't accept identifiers.

    var kstack_bases:  [32]u64
    var kstack_in_use: [32]bool
    var kstack_count = 0

Initialize the slab. Called from `kernel_init` after `vm_init`
and before any thread creation. Drains 32 contiguous 4-page
runs from the bump pool. Asserts adjacency on every page — a
mismatch means the bump pool was already touched, which is a
kernel bug.

    kstack_init()
        var i = 0
        while i < 32
            val p1 = page_alloc_zero()
            val p2 = page_alloc_zero()
            val p3 = page_alloc_zero()
            val p4 = page_alloc_zero()
            if p1 == u64(0) || p2 == u64(0) || p3 == u64(0) || p4 == u64(0)
                abort()
            if p2 != p1 + u64(PAGE_SIZE)
                abort()
            if p3 != p1 + u64(2 * PAGE_SIZE)
                abort()
            if p4 != p1 + u64(3 * PAGE_SIZE)
                abort()
            kstack_bases[i] = p1
            kstack_in_use[i] = false
            i += 1
        kstack_count = 32

Allocate one 16KB kstack. Returns the base PA of the lowest page
(caller computes `kstack_top = base + 4*PAGE_SIZE`). Returns 0
if the pool is exhausted. IRQ-disabled around the bitmap walk
so concurrent allocs from the kernel and a slow-path syscall
handler can't both grab the same slot.

The chunk is zeroed before being handed back: the previous
tenant left stale registers, return addresses, SP_EL0/SP_EL1
slots, etc. on the kstack pages, and a few exception-return
paths read saved-frame slots whose contents the new tenant
hasn't fully written. Pre-chunk-3.5 the linear-scan allocator
went through `page_alloc_zero`, so each page was zero on alloc;
preserve that behaviour here so kstack reuse is observationally
identical to fresh allocation. Zeroing happens after dropping
the IRQ guard — the slot is already marked in-use, so the
16KB byte loop runs with interrupts free.

    kstack_alloc() -> u64
        val prev = arch_irq_save()
        var i = 0
        while i < kstack_count
            if !kstack_in_use[i]
                kstack_in_use[i] = true
                arch_irq_restore(prev)
                val base = kstack_bases[i]
                var p: *byte = *byte(base)
                var j = 0
                while j < 4 * PAGE_SIZE
                    p[j] = byte(0)
                    j += 1
                return base
            i += 1
        arch_irq_restore(prev)
        u64(0)

Free a kstack by base PA. No-op if the base doesn't match any
slot (which would indicate a bug in the caller).

    kstack_free(base: u64)
        val prev = arch_irq_save()
        var i = 0
        while i < kstack_count
            if kstack_bases[i] == base
                kstack_in_use[i] = false
                arch_irq_restore(prev)
                return
            i += 1
        arch_irq_restore(prev)
